                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "A pretty enjoyable yuri VN that took me just under 9 hours to complete. I'll list down some pros and cons and hopefully you can see if it's up your alley.\n\nPros:\n1. The girls are cute with clean, distinctive designs. I occasionally wished the models were higher res as it does look a bit blocky when the camera zooms in, especially towards the MC's face, but this is a minor nitpick.\n2. The game does a good job in setting the mood of a lazy, tropical island. As someone who's visited a good number of such places for vacation, I could definitely relate to the initial excitement of being in paradise, to the boredom of exhausting its limited points of interest and realizing there's nothing left to do, to an eventual appreciation for a carefree lifestyle and an \"enjoy life one day a time\" mentality.\n3. The writing is good. I got the feels a few times and had some good laughs. I enjoyed all the routes with one glaring exception. I'll write the general issues I had with the writing later on in the cons section.\n4. Some of the tracks are pretty nice with the standout being the main theme sung beautifully by its artist. The music isn't perfect though as you'll see in the next part.\n5. I appreciated that there were a good number of choices in the game and the relationship tracker definitely made things more interesting.\n\nCons:\n1. The CGs are pretty limited and all the girls seem to have really small heads in them lol. The one CG where the MC and a love interest is swimming together is genuinely awful IMO and the scene where they go dancing and driving made me howl because of how awkward it all looked. Definitely the game could've benefited from more art. Also, what's with not being able to just see the full CGs in the gallery without it panning everywhere and the text box blocking everything? Also, why is the MC in the foreground hiding your love interest behind her back in not one but TWO CGs? This decision genuinely baffles me.\n2. I found some aspects of the plot really weak like how the MC's family drama and also how the main conflict of the story just resolves itself with minimal input from the player.\n3. The writing can occasionally be awkward. Sure, there's the random typo here and there but more than that, characters would repeat words or phrases uttered by someone totally different as if the VN writer forgot to crosscheck what they wrote. Also, some of the characterization, especially Shiori and a few of the islanders, are cringe. Shiori is just somebody's mental image of what a Japanese girl would be like and some of the islanders will have a stereotypical pidgin accent while all the main cast, including the local girls, speaks in perfect American English. Also this one scene where one dude starts not so subtly talking about real world politics hurt my brain. Annie is best girl though lol (sorry, Mia).\n4. There are some tracks that are a real miss for me. Just the most generic \"tropical\" music out there. Also, there would occasionally be a long span of time without any music at all.\n5. I noticed Sybil changing clothes a few times mid-conversation and the game crashed on me twice during startup.\n\nConclusion:\nI really don't mean to sound overly harsh in my critique and I think even with the issues it has, Summer in Trigue is certainly worth a try for someone who enjoys the genre. It's good fun, it has its moments and I'm keen to see what the devs make next.",
